Consider the simplest case of a regular hexagon  $ABCDEF$  tiled by  $3$  parallelograms - we will use this to introduce some notation. Call a *happy  parallelogram* one whose sides are parallel to  $AB$  and  $BC$ , an *excited parallelogram* one whose sides are parallel to  $CD$  and  $DE$ , and a *joyful parallelogram* one whose sides are parallel to  $EF$  and  $FA$ . Take a line, parallel to  $AB$ , that contains a side of either a *happy parallelogram* or an *excited parallelogram*, partitioning the existing two types into smaller ones of the same type. Note that the sums of these top edges is equal to the length  $AB$ , but that the sums are also equal to the bottom edges of the finite row of smaller parallelograms. Hence, the total area of *happy* and *joyful* parallelograms is  $AB \cdot BD = \frac{2}{3} [ABCDEF]$ , implying that *excited parallelograms* cover a total of  $\frac{1}{3}$  of the area of the hexagon. As such, there must be an equal number of these  $3$  types, as each has the same area.  $\quad \blacksquare$

