For  $k=2,3,...,100$ , we have
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{k^2-2}{k!}
&= \dfrac{k^2}{k!}-\dfrac{2}{k!}
&= \dfrac{k}{(k-1)!}-\dfrac{2}{k!}
&= \dfrac{k-1}{(k-1)!}+\dfrac{1}{(k-1)!}-\dfrac{2}{k!}
&= \dfrac{1}{(k-2)!}+\dfrac{1}{(k-1)!}-\dfrac{2}{k!}.
\end{align*}
So
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=2}^{100} \dfrac{k^2-2}{k!}
&= \sum_{k=2}^{100} \left[\dfrac{1}{(k-2)!}+\dfrac{1}{(k-1)!}-\dfrac{2}{k!}\right]
&= \sum_{k=0}^{98} \dfrac{1}{k!}+\sum_{k=1}^{99} \dfrac{1}{k!}-2\sum_{k=2}^{100} \dfrac{1}{k!}
&= \dfrac{1}{0!}+2\times\dfrac{1}{1!}-\dfrac{1}{99!}-2\times\dfrac{1}{100!}
&< 3.
\end{align*}

