It is always possible to cut the paper into  $3n+1$  rectangles: below is an example of how to do so.
[asy]
filldraw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,10)--(0,10)--cycle, lightgray, black);
filldraw((2,2)--(2,4)--(4,4)--(4,2)--cycle, white, black);
filldraw((1,5)--(1,7)--(3,7)--(3,5)--cycle, white, black);
filldraw((5,7)--(8,7)--(8,8)--(5,8)--cycle, white, black);
filldraw((6,3)--(8,3)--(8,6)--(6,6)--cycle, white, black);
draw((1,0)--(1,10), dashed);
draw((2,0)--(2,5), dashed);
draw((3,4)--(3,10), dashed);
draw((4,0)--(4,10), dashed);
draw((5,0)--(5,10), dashed);
draw((6,0)--(6,7), dashed);
draw((8,0)--(8, 10), dashed);
[/asy]
In words: for each of the  $2n$  vertical edges in one of the  $n$  holes, we cut upwards from the vertical edge until we reach a horizontal edge of a hole (or the edge of the paper); then, we cut downwards from the same vertical edge in the same way. The reason why every shape is a rectangle is because there are no  $270^{\circ}$  angles after we make all the cuts.

We can count the total number of rectangles in this diagram using Euler's characteristic formula. For each of the  $2n$ <details><summary>*</summary>Some cuts may coincide with each other; however, this is obviously not a problem, as it only reduces the number of rectangles produced from our cutting.</details> vertical edges that we make a cut, we create  $4$  vertices in our graph; counting the four vertices of the paper, we have a total of  $8n+4$  vertices. To count the number of edges, notice that every vertex in our graph besides the four corners of the paper have degree  $3$ : from this, we can compute that there are  $12n+4$  edges in our graph. So, there are
\[ 2 + (12n+4) - (8n+4) = 2+4n\]
faces in our graph; however,  $n$  of these are the holes in the rectangle, and  $1$  of them is the infinite face outside the paper. Subtracting these off gives us our desired  $3n+1$  faces.

